comments.message:
Thanks for your review and catching the resource retention issue.

Addressed in the latest patchset, but with clear() + shrink_to_fit() rather than
erase(). Erasing the map entry from writeFruCache() is unsafe because that function runs inside sdbusplus::Timer::timeoutHandler() (see sdbusplus/include/sdbusplus/timer.hpp). After invoking userCallBack(), timeoutHandler() continues to access eventSource:

    if (userCallBack)
    {
        userCallBack();   // <- our writeFruCache() runs here
    }

    int running;
    if (sd_event_source_get_enabled(eventSource, &running) < 0 ||
        running == SD_EVENT_ONESHOT)
    {
        stop();           // <- also touches eventSource
    }

Erasing the map entry inside the callback would destroy the Timer mid-dispatch, freeing eventSource while the dispatcher is still using it — a Use-After-Free.

Compromise: Added releaseWriteCtx() helper that does clear() + shrink_to_fit() 
on the data buffer, releasing the dominant memory cost but keeping the Timer
object.

On unbounded growth: the map is keyed by uint8_t fruDeviceId, and after fixing the validation order, entries are only inserted for IDs that exist in deviceHashes. Map size is therefore bounded by the platform's FRU topology, not unbounded.

